---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../data/site";
import { a0 } from "../data/a0_results";
const k = (x: number) => Math.round(x).toLocaleString("en-AU");
const f1 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(1);
const f2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const pct = Math.round(a0.loss_reduction_vs_naive_pct);
const t2 = (kg: number) => (kg / 1000).toFixed(2);
const fig = (name: string) => href(`/figures/a0/${name}.png`);
const figw = (name: string) => href(`/figures/a0/${name}.webp`);
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
  name: "Study A0 — Profitable nitrogen rates for barley by site, and where more testing pays (simulated)",
  description: "A simulation study of barley farms at real locations, where the right answer is set in advance: the orchestra stops using the crop model where the field data disagree with it, and roughly halves the money lost against a recommendation from the crop model alone.",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Max Moldovan", identifier: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9680-8474" },
  datePublished: "2026-08-26",
};
---
<Base title="Study" description="Study A0, on simulated barley farms at real locations: the orchestra stops using the crop model where the field data disagree with it, prices each nitrogen rate, and cuts the money lost by about half against a recommendation from the crop model alone. Every number on the page is read from the study's computed results." path="/a0" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="kicker">Study A0 · simulated farms (digital twin) · version 0.2 · 2026-08-26 · cause-and-effect check added 2026-10-02</p>
      <h1>Profitable nitrogen rates for barley by site, and where more testing pays (simulated)</h1>
      <p class="prose lead mt-2">For each site in each season (a site-season) of a barley-growing region:
        which nitrogen rate gives the highest expected profit after paying for the nitrogen and for its
        nitrous-oxide emissions, how confident can we be, where should the crop model's advice be set aside,
        and what is one soil test or one strip trial worth? This page reports the first completed study of
        the series. It runs on simulated data in which the right answer was set in advance, so we can check
        whether the methods find it. It is not a field result.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">The set-up</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><strong>The simulated region.</strong> {a0.n_sites} sites at real grain-belt locations with real
        climate (NASA POWER) and soil (SoilGrids) data, five seasons, six nitrogen rates, three varieties and
        three replicates, with yields simulated by the APSIM Next Gen crop model.</li>
      <li><strong>The answer set in advance.</strong> At {a0.n_constrained_sites} sites a hidden subsoil
        constraint caps yield, so yield stops rising above 60 kg N/ha. The calibrated crop model is not given
        this information.</li>
      <li><strong>The chain.</strong> APSIM, run through apsimx, produces the true yields → quorum builds a fast
        stand-in for the crop model and PESTO calibrates it to the field data → kernR's TACI test (a method in
        development) asks whether the field data still agree with the calibrated model → where they do not,
        a curve fitted to the field data alone, one that can level off, takes over → decideR prices each rate:
        the expected loss, the value of perfect information (EVPI) and the value of one more sample (EVSI) →
        optimix picks which sites to test → grainPlan writes the plan for the grower. Each package hands its
        result on in the manifest, the shared result record every package writes. When a package gives no
        result, it records why, and the next package reads that record.</li>
      <li><strong>The economics.</strong> Barley at {f1(a0.price_barley_aud_t)} AUD/t, nitrogen at {f2(a0.cost_per_kgn_aud)} AUD/kg N, plus a notional cost for the nitrous oxide it releases, based on IPCC default values.</li>
      <li><strong>The checks.</strong> Six independent checks run inside the study scripts, each comparing a
        result with a reference result the methods did not produce. Every number in the report is read from a
        computed result, not typed by hand. The whole chain is run again on a second simulated region,
        generated with a different random seed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Three numbers</h2>
    <div class="studies">
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Money lost against the best possible rate</p>
        <h3>{k(a0.loss_cascade_total_aud_ha)} vs {k(a0.loss_naive_total_aud_ha)} AUD/ha</h3>
        <p class="prose">Summed over 198 site-seasons, the full sequence of packages
          against the rate the crop model alone recommends: <strong>{pct} per cent less loss</strong>. Where the constraint was planted, {f1(a0.loss_cascade_constrained_mean)} vs {f1(a0.loss_naive_constrained_mean)} AUD/ha per site-season; elsewhere, {f1(a0.loss_cascade_unconstrained_mean)} vs {f1(a0.loss_naive_unconstrained_mean)}. The second simulated region gives the same order
          ({k(a0.rerun_loss_cascade_total)} vs {k(a0.rerun_loss_naive_total)}).</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Nitrogen and emissions</p>
        <h3>{k(a0.n_saved_vs_naive_kgn)} kg N not applied · {f1(a0.co2e_saved_vs_naive_t_ha)} t CO₂-e/ha avoided</h3>
        <p class="prose">Curves that can level off, fitted to the field data, find the constraint the
          crop model cannot see: the likely range for the rate at which yield levels off contains the planted
          60 kg N/ha at {a0.plateau_interval_covers_60_n} of {a0.n_constrained_sites} sites with the constraint
          (median {Math.round(a0.x0_median_constrained)} vs {Math.round(a0.x0_median_unconstrained)} kg N/ha elsewhere). The TACI test rejects the crop model with sensitivity {f2(a0.taci_sensitivity)} (the share of
          constrained cases it rejects) and specificity {f2(a0.taci_specificity)} (the share of unconstrained cases it accepts).</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">Where the next research dollar pays</p>
        <h3>{a0.n_evsi_priced} of {a0.n_sites} sites priced · top site {Math.round(a0.evsi_top_aud_ha)} AUD/ha for one strip trial</h3>
        <p class="prose">For each site, the expected value of sample information (EVSI): what one strip
          trial or one soil nitrate test is worth before it is done. The result is a map of where the next test
          pays. The estimate reweights a limited number of simulated draws, which caps its precision.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">The four panels</h2>
    <figure class="mt-1">
      <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_poster_panels")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_poster_panels")} width="1600" height="1080" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Four panels. A: a map of the forty sites on real grain-belt coordinates coloured by the method the full sequence of packages used, with the constrained sites ringed. B: yield-response curves at four example sites showing the rate from the crop model alone, the rate from the full sequence of packages and the truly best rate. C: bar charts of mean money lost under each rule, split by whether the constraint was planted. D: a scatter of growing-season rainfall against clay content coloured by the expected value of sample information." /></picture>
      <figcaption class="muted">A: where the crop model was rejected and the field-data fit took over. B: what each method recommends at four sites chosen by a stated rule. C: what each approach loses against the best possible rate. D: where the next research dollar pays.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="mt-2">
      <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_stat_curves")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_stat_curves")} width="1600" height="1028" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Forty small panels, one per site, each showing the observed plot-mean yields at six nitrogen rates with the fitted mean and ninety per cent band from the field-data fit; constrained sites flatten at sixty kilograms of nitrogen per hectare." /></picture>
      <figcaption class="muted">The field-data fit for each site: a blend of linear-plateau and quadratic-plateau random-effects curves fitted to the observed means, each curve weighted by how well it predicts data left out of the fit. This is the range of likely yields the decision step uses wherever the crop model is rejected.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="studies mt-2">
      <figure>
        <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_policy_ledger")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_policy_ledger")} width="1600" height="943" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Bar chart of mean money lost per site-season for four rules: the crop model alone; no nitrogen where no recommendation is given; the district's usual rate where no recommendation is given; and the full sequence of packages. Bars are split by whether the constraint was planted; the full sequence is lowest or near-lowest in both." /></picture>
        <figcaption class="muted">What each fallback rule (what to apply when no recommendation is given) costs, and where.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_plateau_recovery")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_plateau_recovery")} width="1600" height="960" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="Per-site median and ninety per cent interval of the estimated rate at which yield levels off, unconstrained sites on the left and constrained sites on the right, with a dashed line at the planted sixty kilograms of nitrogen per hectare." /></picture>
        <figcaption class="muted">The rate at which yield levels off, estimated from the field data, against the planted 60 kg N/ha at the sites with the constraint.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Cause and effect</h2>
    <p class="prose">A nitrogen decision needs the yield gained by applying a rate. In this study every
      site gets every rate, so a plain comparison of plot yields measures that gain: at 150 kg N/ha it
      matches the planted gain of {t2(a0.causal_truth_150)} t/ha to within{" "}
      {(Math.abs(a0.causal_trial_150 - a0.causal_truth_150) / 1000 + 0.005).toFixed(2)} t/ha.
      Farm records are different. Wetter sites tend to get more nitrogen, and wetter sites also yield more
      (across the simulated region, rainfall and unfertilised yield have a correlation of {f2(a0.causal_cor_gsr_y0)}).
      A plain comparison then credits nitrogen with yield that rainfall produced.</p>
    <p class="prose">To show the size of that error, {a0.causal_n_samples} farm-style samples were drawn
      from the same {a0.causal_n_units} site-season-variety units: one rate per unit, with higher rates more
      likely at wetter sites. The yield gained from nitrogen was then read three ways and compared with
      the planted gain.</p>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><strong>Naive comparison:</strong> {t2(a0.causal_naive_150)} t/ha at 150 kg N/ha (80 per cent of
        samples between {t2(a0.causal_naive_q10_150)} and {t2(a0.causal_naive_q90_150)}), about{" "}
        {f1(a0.causal_naive_ratio_150)} times the planted gain.</li>
      <li><strong>Adjusted for rainfall:</strong> {t2(a0.causal_backdoor_150)} t/ha (all three values are
        medians over the samples). The units are split into
        five rainfall bands, the gain is measured within each band, and the bands are averaged.</li>
      <li><strong>proxymix's mixture model:</strong> {t2(a0.causal_mixture_150)} t/ha. proxymix fits one
        mixture of bell curves to yield, nitrogen and rainfall together; from it, the expected yield with
        and without nitrogen is computed at each unit's own rainfall, and the differences are averaged.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="mt-1">
      <picture><source srcset={figw("fig_causal_layer")} type="image/webp" /><img src={fig("fig_causal_layer")} width="1600" height="1120" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Line chart of extra yield over no nitrogen against nitrogen rate. The planted response rises to about 1.3 tonnes per hectare at 150 kilograms of nitrogen. The naive comparison rises to about 2.8. The rainfall-adjusted and mixture-model lines stay close to the planted response, with shaded bands for the spread across samples." /></picture>
      <figcaption class="muted">Median over {a0.causal_n_samples} farm-style samples; bands hold 80 per cent of them. The adjusted lines stay near the planted gain; at other rates an error of up to {t2(a0.causal_backdoor_bias_max)} t/ha remains.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2 class="mt-3">What it does not show</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li>The region is simulated, not real. The study shows patterns (which sites respond, where a constraint
        flattens the curve), not real-world dollar amounts.</li>
      <li>Two of the six independent checks fail and are reported as failures. The first asks whether the
        estimated levelling-off rate separates sites with and without the constraint; it does not, because
        yield at unconstrained sites levels off too, so that rate alone cannot tell "levels off" from "has the
        constraint". The second is a site-level test on the second simulated region that had too little data
        to detect the effect.</li>
      <li>Version 0.1 of this study lost to the crop model alone: its field-data fit was a straight line in
        nitrogen rate, so it could only ever recommend the lowest or the highest rate tried.</li>
      <li>The value-of-information figures are a ranking rather than exact dollars. They rest on assumed prices for the
        tests and for carbon, and on an assumed district nitrogen rate.</li>
      <li>The cause-and-effect check was set before its reported run to pass only if both adjusted readings
        came within 15 per cent of the planted gain at every rate. It fails: the rainfall adjustment is{" "}
        {Math.round(a0.causal_backdoor_relerr_max * 100)} per cent high at its worst rate and the mixture model{" "}
        {Math.round(a0.causal_mixture_relerr_max * 100)} per cent, both at 30 kg N/ha, where the gain is smallest.
        At 120 kg N/ha the rainfall adjustment reads more than at 150, so its close match at 150 is partly chance.
        The rainfall adjustment also gives no answer for {a0.causal_backdoor_declined} of its {a0.causal_n_samples * 5} readings, where a
        rainfall band had no plot at a rate. The farm-style samples are drawn from the simulated trial, so the
        size of the gap depends on how strongly the samples tie nitrogen to rainfall. Rainfall is the only
        thing that sets the rate in these samples, so adjusting for it is enough by design; on real farms soil,
        rotation and the grower also shape the rate, and each would have to be measured.</li>
      <li>No GRDC-origin data enters the simulated region, the figures or this page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Reproduce it</h2>
    <p class="prose">The study is one control script that runs seven stage scripts in turn, and an eighth that adds the cause-and-effect check. Each stage is a
      standalone R script with its own independent check. Read them in this order:</p>
    <ol class="prose">
      <li><strong>Generate the region.</strong> Sample {a0.n_sites} sites on real grain-belt coordinates, collect NASA POWER
        climate and SoilGrids soil, run APSIM barley for every site, season, variety and rate, then plant the hidden
        subsoil constraint at {a0.n_constrained_sites} sites after the simulation has run.</li>
      <li><strong>Calibrate and test.</strong> Train a fast stand-in for the crop model for each site-season, calibrate it
        with PESTO on the low rates (0 to 60 kg N/ha), and let kernR test, on the higher rates, whether the field
        data still agree with the calibrated model.</li>
      <li><strong>Fit curves to the field data.</strong> Fit linear-plateau and quadratic-plateau curves with brms on all
        plots, weight them by how well each predicts plots left out of the fit, and pass the range of likely yields at
        each site to the next stage.</li>
      <li><strong>Decide and value.</strong> Price every candidate rate by expected profit with decideR, using the
        model where kernR accepted it and the field-record fit where it did not; price one soil test and one strip
        trial per site; write the grainPlan nitrogen plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Report.</strong> Read every number on this page and in the report from a computed result.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-verify.</strong> Run the whole chain again on the second simulated region.</li>
      <li><strong>Figure and summary.</strong> Draw the four panels and write the table that links each poster number to its computed result.</li>
      <li><strong>Cause and effect.</strong> Draw the farm-style samples and read the gain from nitrogen three ways against the planted gain.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="prose">The scripts are released as one archive with a README that lists what each stage reads and writes, alongside the study tree.
      The generated data are not shipped: stage 01 regenerates them from the public sources named above.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Take it with you</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><a href={href("/downloads/a0_poster.pdf")}>The symposium poster</a> (PDF, A0 portrait, version 6 of 2026-10-02; every number is read from the same table as this page).</li>
      <li><a href={href("/downloads/orchestra_brief.pdf")}>A one-page briefing note</a> (PDF) for research managers and data custodians.</li>
      <li><a href={href("/downloads/a0_summary.md")}>The one-page study summary</a> (Markdown).</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/members")}>The packages</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/about")}>About</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
